/-!
# The Skorokhod space

Càdlàg paths on `[0,T]` with values in a metric space, time changes of `[0,T]` and
Billingsley's J1 metric, with the characterization of J1 convergence by time changes; continuous
paths as a closed subspace on which J1 is uniform; evaluation, restriction, constant extension
and stopping, continuous at paths continuous at the cut time; time–space scaling, continuous at
`(f, 1, 1)` when `f` is continuous at the horizon; the largest positive and absolute jumps as
2-Lipschitz functionals; and
Billingsley's equivalent complete metric `d°`: the Skorokhod space of a complete metric space
is completely metrizable. For separable `E` the Skorokhod space is separable, its Borel
σ-algebra is generated by evaluations at a dense set of times containing the terminal time, laws
are determined by finite-dimensional distributions, and a law has countably many fixed jump times.
Convergence in law follows from convergence of
the restrictions to `[0, a]` for `a ↑ T` plus control of the terminal oscillation, and laws a.s.
continuous at `T` are determined by their restrictions. The càdlàg Vervaat map rotates a path
at its first infimum time; it is measurable, matches the discrete Vervaat rotation of words on
step paths, and is J1-continuous at paths with a unique interior infimum time. Billingsley's modulus
`w'` gives a compactness criterion and
tightness criteria for laws on the Skorokhod space.
These results do not depend on trees.
-/
